@@ -50,6 +50,10 @@ A PR watch (`watches.ts`) is a `WatchDoc` in its session and a durable task ther
 
 A repository watch (`repo-watches.ts`) is an item of the app-wide `RepoWatchesDoc` and a durable task in the conversation it messages (PiP's, or a session's), with the same wait, look, and send phases as a PR watch: a look puts the message in the task's checkpoint, and the send's request id is the task's and its count's, so it goes once. It reads the repository's issues, pulls, workflow runs, releases, and comments through `gh api`, and keeps the ids it told. PiP's tools (`repo_watch`, `session_watch_pr`, `session_start` with `repo`) act through `Pip` as its person; a repository is worked on or watched only where `gh`'s account can push, and `Pip.checkout` finds a session's checkout whose `origin` is the repository, or clones it once into `repos/`.
 
+### Optional agent-message slash bridge
+
+`/agent-msgs on|off|status` is handled in the browser and calls `Commands.agentMsgs` through `POST /api/c/:id/agent-msgs`, not `submit`. The command checks owner/access/session permissions and resolves only the fixed `pi-agent-msgs.reporting-command` task from the enabled registry (`ExtensionLoader.registeredTask`). It creates a background task and returns its status; it never executes arbitrary tools or starts a generation, so it also works without a selected model or after a spend limit. The external drop-in shares this state-changing implementation with its conversational tool. `hello.server.agentMsgsSlash` keeps newer web code from invoking the bridge before the server has restarted. This is not a general extension slash-command registration API.
+
 ## Who chooses models
 
 Only people choose models; Pi, PiP, and Pi Pocket itself never pick one on the side. Everything that runs a model uses one a person chose:
